[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: CSW1’s OSPF adjacency toward R1 on interface eth0 (CD_WAN_LINK_1). The OSPF process itself and costs are fine; the issue is the interface/link state.

Mechanism: When CSW1’s eth0 went down, OSPF on that interface stopped sending/receiving hellos, tearing down the neighbor with R1. R1 still runs OSPF on its eth0, but with no peer on the segment it remains alone (DR with zero neighbors). With only the R1–CSW2 adjacency up, R1’s ECMP reduced from two paths to one.

Localization: On CSW1, “show ip ospf neighbor” shows no neighbor on eth0 and lists only bond0/eth3–eth6. “show ip ospf interface” does not list eth0 at all. CSW1 interface state shows eth0 DOWN (ip addr: state DOWN; ifconfig lacks UP/RUNNING). ethtool on csw1 eth0 reports “Link detected: no.” Conversely, R1’s “show ip ospf neighbor” shows a single neighbor on eth1 (to CSW2), and “show ip ospf interface” shows eth0 up with no neighbor. CSW1’s OSPF config still includes “network 10.0.0.32/30 area 0,” so no OSPF configuration/ cost change removed the interface from OSPF.

Root cause: CSW1’s WAN-facing interface eth0 was left down/disconnected after the maintenance, not an OSPF configuration change.

Corrective fix: Restore CSW1 eth0 on CD_WAN_LINK_1 (enable the interface and verify cabling), then confirm OSPF hellos/neighborship with R1 form. If eth0 was intentionally superseded by new bonding, migrate R1’s link accordingly or revert to a standalone, up interface. === END ===